export const meta = {
  name: 'deploy-topology-rootcause',
  description: 'Adversarially verify root causes for broken deploy topology / tags / semver / previews, and the "QA serves two versions at once" symptom',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Investigate', detail: 'one agent per hypothesis + open-ended sweeps' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'three adversarial lenses per candidate finding' },
  ],
}

const REGISTRY = 'ghcr.io/nomos-n4s/apivo-news'

const CONTEXT = `
REPO: C:\\Users\\madym\\repos\\apivo-news — Go API + Astro (SSR, @astrojs/node standalone) frontend.

DEPLOYMENT TOPOLOGY (established facts, verified by reading the files):
- Registry ${REGISTRY}/api and /web. Channels are MUTABLE TAGS: qa, staging, prod. Immutable tags: sha-<commit>. Preview tags: pr-<n>.
- .github/workflows/publish.yml: push to main -> version "main-<short sha>", channel qa. workflow_call from release.yml -> version=<tag>, channel=staging|prod.
  It builds api (context .) and web (context web), pushes each under sha-<sha> with OCI labels org.opencontainers.image.version/revision/source, proves the api image reports its version via 'docker run ... version', THEN moves the channel with TWO SEPARATE calls:
    docker buildx imagetools create --tag $REGISTRY/api:$CHANNEL $REGISTRY/api@$API_DIGEST
    docker buildx imagetools create --tag $REGISTRY/web:$CHANNEL $REGISTRY/web@$WEB_DIGEST
- deploy/hetzner/bin/apivo-reconcile: systemd timer per env, every minute. Resolves api:<channel> and web:<channel> to digests via 'buildx imagetools inspect', compares to /var/lib/apivo/<env>/current, pulls both by digest, reads org.opencontainers.image.version off BOTH images (image_version(), falling back to the literal string 'dev' when the label is missing), refuses to roll out if the two differ ("version_skew"), writes /var/lib/apivo/<env>/images.env with APIVO_API_IMAGE, APIVO_WEB_IMAGE, APIVO_VERSION=<the API image's label>, runs 'compose up -d --remove-orphans --wait', verifies each container's .Config.Image equals the pinned digest ref, then execs into the web container to fetch the api's /healthz and requires the served version to equal the api image's label. Failure rolls back to the recorded previous digests.
- deploy/hetzner/compose/docker-compose.yml: services api and web, FIXED container_name apivo-<env>-api / apivo-<env>-web, no depends_on between them, restart: unless-stopped. web gets PUBLIC_APP_VERSION: \${APIVO_VERSION:-dev} as a RUNTIME env var (the web image itself carries no compiled-in version).
- The Go api stamps main.version via -ldflags from a git-ignored VERSION file in the build context; /healthz reports it. The web reports PUBLIC_APP_VERSION (astro:env/server) in its footer, and web/src/lib/cashback/api.ts BRANCHES ON IT: isPreviewVersion(pr-<digits>) switches the cashback surfaces to fixtures.
- Caddy (deploy/hetzner/caddy/): one upstream per environment, apivo-<env>-api:8080 for '/api/v1/* /healthz /readyz', apivo-<env>-web:4321 (over TLS, insecure_skip_verify) for everything else. Previews at https://*.\${APIVO_PREVIEW_DOMAIN} with @preview header_regexp ^(pr-[0-9]+)\\. -> apivo-pr-<n>-api / apivo-pr-<n>-web.
- deploy/hetzner/bin/apivo-previews: polls pr-* tags every minute; preview containers run MUTABLE TAGS (api:pr-<n>) not digests; create uses 'up -d --wait', the converge branch uses 'up -d' with NO --wait.
- deploy/hetzner/environments.env: APIVO_QA_URL=https://ra1ze.com, APIVO_STAGING_URL= (EMPTY), APIVO_PROD_URL= (EMPTY), APIVO_PREVIEW_DOMAIN=ra1ze.com (SAME domain as QA).
- release.yml refuses a release to a channel whose URL is empty, before publishing anything.
- Git facts: the ONLY tag in the repo is v0.1.0 (2026-08-16); origin/main is 1072 commits ahead of it. So staging and prod have never been released.
- deploy/hetzner/** (Caddyfiles, compose files, systemd units, apivo-reconcile, apivo-previews) is installed onto hosts by deploy/hetzner/provision.sh, run BY HAND. Verify whether ANY automation ships those files to a running host.

USER'S REPORTED SYMPTOMS: "deployment topology, tags, semver, preview environments and everything related to it" are broken. "The only thing working is QA and sometimes it serves 2 VERSIONS AT THE SAME TIME."

Read the actual files. Cite file:line for every claim. Never invent a line number.
`

const FINDINGS = {
  type: 'object',
  additionalProperties: false,
  required: ['findings'],
  properties: {
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        additionalProperties: false,
        required: ['title', 'file', 'line', 'mechanism', 'symptom', 'severity', 'confidence'],
        properties: {
          title: { type: 'string' },
          file: { type: 'string' },
          line: { type: 'integer' },
          mechanism: { type: 'string', description: 'exact causal chain, quoting the code' },
          symptom: { type: 'string', description: 'what an operator/user observes' },
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['critical', 'high', 'medium', 'low'] },
          confidence: { type: 'string', enum: ['certain', 'likely', 'speculative'] },
          fix: { type: 'string' },
        },
      },
    },
  },
}

const VERDICT = {
  type: 'object',
  additionalProperties: false,
  required: ['refuted', 'reason'],
  properties: {
    refuted: { type: 'boolean' },
    reason: { type: 'string' },
    correction: { type: 'string', description: 'if partially right, the accurate version' },
  },
}

const LINES = [
  {
    key: 'two-versions',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

YOUR JOB: find EVERY mechanism by which the QA environment (https://ra1ze.com) can serve TWO DIFFERENT VERSIONS AT THE SAME TIME, or report two different versions from two surfaces. This is the user's loudest symptom and the highest-value question. Be exhaustive and mechanical.

Consider and CHECK IN THE CODE at minimum:
1. The api/web split. Two images, two mutable channel tags moved in two separate registry calls (publish.yml). What windows exist where api:qa and web:qa point at different builds? What does apivo-reconcile's version_skew guard actually cover, and what does it NOT cover? What happens if the web image's OCI version label is missing or malformed — trace image_version()'s 'dev' fallback and what {{index .Config.Labels "..."}} prints for an ABSENT label (hint: Go templates print '<no value>', not empty — verify what that does to the comparison).
2. The rollout window. docker compose up -d --wait with FIXED container_name: can the api and web containers be replaced at different times? Is there any ordering, drain, or readiness gate coupling them? What does Caddy do during the gap? Quantify the window.
3. The two reporting surfaces. /healthz reports the api binary's compiled main.version. The footer reports PUBLIC_APP_VERSION, a RUNTIME env var set from APIVO_VERSION, which apivo-reconcile sets from the API image's label — never the web image's. Enumerate every state in which those two disagree. Is there ANY path by which the running web bundle's identity can be observed?
4. Half-moved channels. If the second 'imagetools create' in publish.yml fails or the job dies between the two calls, what is the resulting steady state on a host? Trace it tick by tick.
5. Concurrent publishes. publish.yml concurrency group 'publish-qa' with cancel-in-progress unset. Two merges close together: can channel tags end up pointing at DIFFERENT commits for api vs web? Reason about GitHub's queue semantics and about imagetools create ordering.
6. Preview stacks and QA sharing a domain (APIVO_PREVIEW_DOMAIN == APIVO_QA_HOST == ra1ze.com) and a host. Can a preview stack ever answer for the QA hostname, or vice versa? Check the Caddy site-block matching order and the @preview matcher's fallthrough.
7. Caching: Cloudflare in front, Astro hashed assets, any Cache-Control the app or Caddy sets. Can an old HTML document + old hashed assets survive a rollout? grep the web app and Caddy for cache headers.
8. Anything else you find. Read deploy/hetzner/compose/docker-compose.edge.yml and docker-compose.local-db.yml too.

Report each distinct mechanism as its own finding.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'config-delivery',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

YOUR JOB: determine EXACTLY what reaches a running host and what does not, and prove it.

The image path is automated (publish.yml moves a channel tag; the host pulls). But deploy/hetzner/** — the Caddyfiles, snippets.caddy, every compose file, the systemd units, and the apivo-reconcile / apivo-previews / apivoctl SCRIPTS THEMSELVES — appear to be installed only by deploy/hetzner/provision.sh, run by hand over ssh.

1. Read deploy/hetzner/provision.sh in full. List every file it installs and where. Does it pull from a git checkout on the host, or from the local working copy of whoever runs it? How would an operator get the current version of these files onto a host?
2. grep EVERY workflow in .github/workflows/ for any step that ships anything to a host (ssh, scp, rsync, ansible, a git pull on the box, a systemd unit that self-updates). Report what you find, or report definitively that nothing does.
3. Establish the DRIFT. Use git log to date the last change to each of: the Caddyfiles/snippets.caddy, docker-compose*.yml, the systemd units, apivo-reconcile, apivo-previews, provision.sh. Then reason about what a host provisioned at some earlier date would be running versus what main says today. Name the specific behaviours that would be MISSING on such a host (e.g. #546's /api/v1/* Caddy routing fix, #547's brand-directory mount, the web TLS certificate requirement, the version_skew guard, the preview pull-before-up fix).
4. Specifically: apivo-reconcile refuses to run without \${APIVO_ETC}/<env>/web-certs/web.crt, and docker-compose.yml mounts \${APIVO_ETC}/<env>/brand. Both are created only by provision.sh. What happens on a host provisioned before those existed? Trace the exact failure and what an operator sees.
5. Is there any versioning, checksum, or drift detection at all between the repo's deploy/hetzner/** and what a host actually has? Does apivoctl status or validate.sh check it?

Report each gap as a finding.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'release-semver',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

YOUR JOB: audit the release / tag / semver path end to end and explain why nothing but QA has ever deployed. Read .github/workflows/release.yml IN FULL, plus scripts/release_guard.sh, release_semver.sh, release_notes.sh, and docs/RELEASING.md.

1. Enumerate every precondition for a successful staging release (an -rc tag) and for a production release. For each, state whether it is satisfied today (APIVO_STAGING_URL is empty; APIVO_PROD_URL is empty; only tag is v0.1.0; a 'production' GitHub Environment with reviewers is required for prod).
2. Trace what happens TODAY if someone runs: git tag -a v0.2.0-rc.1 -m 'rc' && git push origin v0.2.0-rc.1. Exactly which job fails, at which line, with what message, and what state is left behind.
3. VERSION file: the Dockerfile reads a git-ignored VERSION at the context root; a stale committed-looking VERSION exists in the working tree (untracked, contents 'v0.1.0'). Check .dockerignore's allowlist and confirm the web build (context: web) has NO version stamping mechanism at all. What is the consequence for the web image's OCI label vs its actual content?
4. Is there ANY check that the api image's label, the api binary's compiled main.version, and the channel/tag agree? Where could they diverge?
5. The release probe: which URL and path does it hit, and does it compare the SERVED version to the tag? Does anything probe the WEB at all?
6. Semver hygiene: is there any automation that bumps a version, generates the next tag, or enforces that main is releasable? Or is cutting a release entirely a manual 'git tag -a'? What is missing to make this routine?
7. Check ci.yml: which gates run on the publish path vs only on pull requests? k8s-topology.yml and make-targets.yml declare workflow_call but note nothing calls them — verify whether publish.yml or release.yml actually call them, and name any gate that protects PRs but NOT the path that deploys to QA.

Report each gap as a finding.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'previews',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

YOUR JOB: audit the preview-environment path end to end and find every way it breaks. Read .github/workflows/preview.yml IN FULL and deploy/hetzner/bin/apivo-previews IN FULL (538 lines), plus docker-compose.preview.yml and docker-compose.preview-db.yml.

1. Trace the full lifecycle: PR opened -> tags pushed -> host converges -> hostname serves. Then PR closed -> tags deleted -> host tears down. Name every step that can fail silently.
2. Preview images are addressed by MUTABLE TAG (api:pr-<n>), not by digest, unlike every named environment. Enumerate the consequences: staleness, the pull-then-up race, what happens when a push moves the tag mid-converge, and whether api and web can end up from different commits inside one preview.
3. The converge branch runs 'up -d' WITHOUT --wait while create runs 'up -d --wait'. What does that cost?
4. The cap: APIVO_PREVIEW_MAX (default 5). What happens to PRs over the cap? Is the selection stable? What does a contributor see?
5. PUBLIC_APP_VERSION for previews: docker-compose.preview.yml defaults to the literal string 'preview' (\${APIVO_PREVIEW_VERSION:-preview}), and web/src/lib/cashback/api.ts's isPreviewVersion explicitly REJECTS 'preview' — only pr-<digits> counts. Find every path where the default could be used instead of pr-<n>, and state the consequence for the cashback surfaces.
6. The preview database: shared Postgres container, a database per PR, tmpfs. Can a preview's migrations or teardown affect QA? Is apivo-preview-postgres on a network reachable from QA? Check copy_qa_editors — it docker-execs into apivo-qa-postgres from the preview reconciler. What does that imply about isolation and about what happens when QA's postgres is absent or renamed?
7. TLS/DNS: previews live at pr-<n>.ra1ze.com. Is there a wildcard DNS record? A wildcard origin certificate? What does provision.sh install, and what must exist in Cloudflare that no repo file can create? Name every manual prerequisite.
8. Does anything delete preview IMAGE TAGS for PRs that were closed while CI was down? Is there a garbage collector for orphaned ghcr tags or preview databases?

Report each gap as a finding.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'open-sweep',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

YOUR JOB: an OPEN-ENDED sweep for deployment defects the four targeted investigations would miss. Do not repeat the obvious; hunt for what is not on anyone's list.

Read with fresh eyes: deploy/hetzner/provision.sh, deploy/hetzner/validate.sh, deploy/hetzner/bin/apivoctl, the systemd units (apivo-reconcile@.service/.timer, apivo-previews.service/.timer, apivo-edge.service), docker-compose.edge*.yml, docker-compose.local-db.yml, docker-compose.cashback.yml, deploy/hetzner/env/*.example, and deploy/k8s/ (is the Kubernetes path dead code that still gates CI?).

Hunt specifically for:
1. systemd correctness: OnCalendar/OnUnitActiveSec intervals, missing RandomizedDelaySec, overlapping runs, Type=oneshot semantics, what happens when a reconcile exceeds the timer interval, whether a failed unit stops retrying, sandboxing (ProtectSystem/ReadOnlyPaths) that could break buildx as the reconcile script's own comment hints at.
2. Locking: is there ANY mutual exclusion between apivo-reconcile@qa, apivo-reconcile@staging and apivo-previews running concurrently on one host? They share a docker daemon, a disk, and 'docker image prune'. Can the prune in apivo-reconcile delete an image a preview is about to start, or vice versa?
3. Resource exhaustion on a single VPS: QA stack + staging stack + up to 5 preview stacks + a preview postgres on tmpfs + Caddy, with memory limits of 512M each. Add it up against a plausible Hetzner box. What breaks first?
4. The state directory /var/lib/apivo/<env>/: can current/previous/images.env get out of sync with the daemon's reality? What happens if images.env exists but the images were pruned? What happens after a host reboot?
5. Naming/agreement bugs: does every file agree on container names, network names, project names, registry paths, channel names, and environment names? Cross-check docker-compose*.yml 'name:' and container_name against what Caddy proxies to, what apivo-reconcile inspects, and what apivo-previews and copy_qa_editors exec into.
6. Anything in deploy/k8s/ that contradicts the Hetzner topology or that CI still gates on for a path nobody deploys.
7. Any place an error is swallowed with '|| true', '2>/dev/null', or a discarded exit status where the failure would be invisible and consequential.

Report each as a finding.`,
  },
]

phase('Investigate')

const results = await pipeline(
  LINES,
  (line) => agent(line.prompt, { label: `investigate:${line.key}`, phase: 'Investigate', schema: FINDINGS }),
  (found, line) => {
    const items = (found && found.findings) || []
    if (!items.length) return []
    log(`${line.key}: ${items.length} candidate findings -> verifying`)
    return parallel(
      items.map((f) => () =>
        parallel(
          [
            `CODE-TRUTH lens: open ${f.file} and read around line ${f.line}. Does the code actually say what this claims? Quote the real lines. If the line number is wrong but the claim holds elsewhere in the file, say so in 'correction' and do NOT refute.`,
            `DEFEATER lens: find the guard, check, test, comment or downstream step that would ALREADY PREVENT this from happening. This codebase is unusually well defended (apivo-reconcile has a version_skew guard, a digest verification, a served-version assertion and a rollback; there are test suites at deploy/hetzner/bin/*_test.sh and scripts/*_test.sh). Search those test suites for a case covering this. If a real defence exists, REFUTE.`,
            `CONSEQUENCE lens: grant the mechanism. Does it actually produce the claimed symptom, at a rate and in a form an operator would notice and describe as broken? A theoretical race that closes in 200ms, or one that self-heals on the next 60-second tick with no user-visible effect, is REFUTED as a cause of the reported symptoms. Judge severity honestly.`,
          ].map((lens, i) => () =>
            agent(
              `${CONTEXT}\n\nCLAIM UNDER TEST\n  title: ${f.title}\n  file: ${f.file}:${f.line}\n  mechanism: ${f.mechanism}\n  claimed symptom: ${f.symptom}\n  claimed severity: ${f.severity}\n\n${lens}\n\nDefault to refuted=true when you cannot substantiate the claim from the code. Be adversarial but fair: a real defect that is correctly described must NOT be refuted.`,
              { label: `verify:${line.key}:${f.title.slice(0, 28)}:${i}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT },
            ),
          ),
        ).then((votes) => {
          const cast = votes.filter(Boolean)
          const kept = cast.filter((v) => !v.refuted).length
          return {
            ...f,
            line_of_inquiry: line.key,
